#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:0
msgid ""
"Debug confined snaps with shells, strace, developer mode, diagnostics, "
"policy-violation logs, AppArmor, seccomp, and device access checks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:8
msgid "Debug snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:10
msgid ""
"Each snap runs inside its own {ref}`confined environment <explanation-"
"security-snap-confinement>`, also called \"sandbox\". The policy of each "
"sandbox describes what the application is allowed to do. When an application "
"tries to do something that is not allowed, the system logs a policy "
"violation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:12
msgid ""
"The following techniques can help you investigate and solve these policy "
"violations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:14
msgid "Use `snap try`  to quickly test changes without rebuilding your snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:15
msgid "Use `snap run --shell` to inspect and test the confined environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:16
msgid "Use developer mode to try your snap without confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:17
msgid "Investigating policy violation logs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:18
msgid ""
"Use `snappy-debug` to investigate violation logs and receive suggestions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:19
msgid "Manually search the raw logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:20
msgid "Research AppArmor violations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:21
msgid "Research seccomp] violations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:22
msgid "Investigate file permissions and cgroup device access violations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:23
msgid ""
"Use GDB and gdbserver from within a snap's environment to isolate and "
"identify potential issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:25
msgid ""
"For more details on how AppArmor, seccomp and device permission security "
"policies are implemented, see {ref}`Security policy and sandboxing "
"<explanation-security-security-policies>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:27
msgid "Run a shell in the confined environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:29
msgid ""
"To investigate and test the confined environment of a snap, you can open a "
"`bash` shell in it. After the snap is installed, use the `--shell  <name>."
"<command>` argument of `snap run`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:31
msgid ""
"$ snap run --shell mysnap.mycommand\n"
"To run a command as administrator (user \"root\"), use \"sudo <command>\".\n"
"See \"man sudo_root\" for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:37
msgid ""
"This will create the confined environment of the Snap, execute the [command-"
"chain](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/reference/snapcraft-"
"yaml/#assumes) and then run `bash` inside that environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:39
msgid ""
"You can then investigate which files your snap has access to by running "
"commands such as `ls` and `cat`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:41
msgid ""
"ⓘ It's important to put `--shell` _before_ the name of the snap. Otherwise "
"it will be interpreted as an argument to the application instead of an "
"argument to `snap run`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:43
msgid "Run a snap under strace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:45
msgid "Note that this requires _snapd 2.62_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:47
msgid ""
"Viewing which [system calls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_call) are "
"made by an application, and how the Linux kernel responds to them,  can be "
"beneficial in gaining insights into a failure.  This can be accomplished "
"with the widely used [strace](https://strace.io/) utility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:49
msgid ""
"Running the standard _strace_ command on a snapped application, however, can "
"produce confusing results due to the {ref}`confined environment <explanation-"
"security-snap-confinement>` most snaps run within. To solve this problem, "
"_snapd_ includes specific support for running an application under strace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:51
msgid ""
"To use this, you first have to install the [strace-static](https://"
"snapcraft.io/strace-static) snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:53
msgid "sudo snap install strace-static\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:57
msgid ""
"With _strace-static_ installed, you can now use the `--strace` argument with "
"the _snap_ command to launch _strace_ within the snap environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:59
msgid "snap run --strace <snap-name>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:63
msgid ""
"You will be asked for your password because the internal strace logic relies "
"on _sudo_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:65
msgid ""
"Additionally, you can disable post-processing of the strace output by "
"passing `--raw` to `--strace=`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:67
msgid "snap run --strace=--raw <snap-name>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:71
msgid ""
"Strace is highly versatile. You can learn more about it by reading the "
"manual page [strace(1)](https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/"
"strace.1.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:73
msgid "Developer mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:75
msgid ""
"To help isolate runtime errors when building and testing a snap, a snap can "
"be installed using _developer mode_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:77
msgid "To install a snap in developer mode, use the `--devmode` argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:79
msgid "sudo snap install --devmode mysnap\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:83
msgid ""
"When a snap is installed with developer mode, violations against a snap's "
"security policy are permitted to proceed but logged via journald."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:85
msgid "Generating a system diagnostic report"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:87
msgid ""
"[`snap-debug-info.sh`](https://github.com/canonical/snapd/blob/master/debug-"
"tools/snap-debug-info.sh) is a script included in the snapd snap that "
"collects comprehensive information about snapd's state. It contains system "
"details that are useful in bug reports as well as diagnosing snapd "
"behaviour. These include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:89
msgid "`snap` command's version,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:90
msgid "active model assertion,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:91
msgid "snaps, components, and services installed,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:92
msgid "connections on the system,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:93
msgid ""
"recent changes performed on the system including those in Doing or Error "
"state,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:94
msgid "offline snap changes,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:95
msgid "validation sets,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:96
msgid "uptime, date, and disk space,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:97
msgid "contents of gadget.yaml (if a gadget snap is present),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:98
msgid "snap system config,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:99
msgid "status of the snapd.service,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:100
msgid "snapd journal entries,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:101
msgid "DENIED log messages from system journal,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:102
msgid "snapd stack traces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:104
msgid ""
"Note that the output is expected to be verbose, especially on systems with "
"many snaps installed. Run the script as root or with sudo since some "
"commands used require those permissions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:106
msgid ""
"sudo /snap/snapd/current/usr/lib/snapd/snap-debug-info.sh > snap-debug.log\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:110
msgid "Debugging policy violation logs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:112
msgid "Using snappy-debug to show violations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:114
msgid ""
"The easiest way to find and fix policy violations is to use [the `snappy-"
"debug` tool](https://snapcraft.io/snappy-debug). It"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:116
msgid "watches syslog for policy violations,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:117
msgid "shows them in a human readable format,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:118
msgid "and makes recommendations for how to solve them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:120
msgid "First, install the tool by running the following command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:122
msgid "sudo snap install snappy-debug\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:126
msgid "Then, run the following command to start watching policy violations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:128
msgid ""
"$ sudo snappy-debug\n"
"INFO: Following '/var/log/syslog'. If have dropped messages, use:\n"
"INFO: $ sudo journalctl --output=short --follow --all | sudo snappy-debug\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:134
msgid "If you have dropped messages, try the following command instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:136
msgid "sudo journalctl --output=short --follow --all | sudo snappy-debug\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:140
msgid ""
"Note: these commands only show policy violations that happen _after_ you run "
"them. So first run one of these commands and then run the snap that you want "
"to debug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:142
msgid "See `snappy-debug --help` for more information about this tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:144
msgid ""
"If you believe there is a bug in a security policy or want to request and/or "
"contribute a new interface, please [file a bug](https://bugs.launchpad.net/"
"snappy/+filebug), adding the `snapd-interface` tag, and feel free to discuss "
"policy issues [on the forum](https://forum.snapcraft.io/c/snapd)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:147
msgid "Manually extracting violation logs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:149
msgid ""
"Note that this method does not show _all_ violation logs, since not all logs "
"contain the term \"audit\" in them. Use `snappy-debug` to see all violation "
"logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:151
msgid ""
"You can also manually show snap policy violations by searching the logs for "
"_audit_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:153
msgid "$ sudo journalctl --since=yesterday | grep audit\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:157
msgid ""
"The above command uses `--since=yesterday` to limit the typically verbose "
"logging output from journalctl."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:159
msgid ""
"A handy debugging technique is to tail/follow journalctl output while "
"exercising the snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:161
msgid ""
"$ sudo sysctl -w kernel.printk_ratelimit=0 ; journalctl --follow | grep "
"audit\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:165
msgid "As shown above, kernel log rate limiting can be disabled manually with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:166
msgid "$ sudo sysctl -w kernel.printk_ratelimit=0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:170
msgid "Understanding AppArmor violations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:172
msgid ""
"An AppArmor violation will look something like the following and include "
"`apparmor=DENIED`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:174
msgid ""
"audit: type=1400 audit(1431384420.408:319): apparmor=\"DENIED\" "
"operation=\"mkdir\" profile=\"snap.foo.bar\" name=\"/var/lib/foo\" pid=637 "
"comm=\"bar\" requested_mask=\"c\" denied_mask=\"c\" fsuid=0 ouid=0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:178
msgid ""
"If there are no AppArmor denials, AppArmor shouldn't be blocking the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:180
msgid ""
"To better understand AppArmor policy for a strictly installed snap, modify "
"the AppArmor policy _in place_ on the target system. Changes aren't "
"persistent, but this can help when considering a snapd patch or bug report."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:182
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:184
msgid "build the  snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:185
msgid ""
"copy the snap to the target device and install it (or use {ref}`snap try "
"<interfaces-snap-try>`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:186
msgid ""
"use the snap, perhaps using `snap run --shell <name>.<command>`, monitoring "
"via journalctl for denials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:187
msgid ""
"modifying `/var/lib/snapd/apparmor/profiles/snap.<name>.<command>` as needed "
"(eg, adding rules before the final `'}'`)and running `sudo apparmor_parser "
"-r /var/lib/snapd/apparmor/profiles/snap.<name>.<command>` to compile and "
"load the policy into the kernel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:188
msgid ""
"use `sudo service snap.<name>.<command> stop/start/etc` as needed for daemons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:189
msgid "repeat until AppArmor policy issues are resolved"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:191
msgid "Understanding seccomp violations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:193
msgid "A seccomp violation will look something like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:195
msgid ""
"audit: type=1326 audit(1430766107.122:16): auid=1000 uid=1000 gid=1000 "
"ses=15 pid=1491 comm=\"env\" exe=\"/bin/bash\" sig=31 arch=40000028 "
"syscall=983045 compat=0 ip=0xb6fb0bd6 code=0x0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:199
msgid ""
"The `syscall=983045` can be resolved by running the `scmp_sys_resolver` "
"command on a system of the same architecture as the one with the seccomp "
"violation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:201
msgid ""
"$ scmp_sys_resolver 983045\n"
"set_tls\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:206
msgid "If there are no seccomp violations, seccomp isn't blocking the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:208
msgid ""
"If you notice `compat=1` in the seccomp denial, then specify the correct "
"compatibility architecture to `scmp_sys_resolver` with `-a <arch>`. For "
"example, when on an amd64 system, use `scmp_sys_resolver -a x86 191` (use `-"
"a arm` on arm64 systems)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:210
msgid ""
"The seccomp filter profile in expected to be located in /var/lib/snapd/"
"seccomp/bpf/*.src (formerly /var/lib/snapd/seccomp/profiles)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:212
msgid ""
"The seccomp profile source (the `*.src` file in the profile directory) needs "
"to be recompiled into the profile binary (`*.bin` in the profile directory) "
"as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:214
msgid ""
"sudo /usr/lib/snapd/snap-seccomp compile /var/lib/snapd/seccomp/bpf/snap."
"$SNAP_NAME.src /var/lib/snapd/seccomp/bpf/snap.$SNAP_NAME.bin\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:218
msgid ""
"The `snap-confine` command will load the bpf in the `.bin` file for the "
"command when you (re)launch the command or `snap run --shell`. The seccomp "
"policy language is considerably simpler and is essentially a list of allowed "
"syscalls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:220
msgid ""
"When done, copy any changes you make to `/var/lib/snapd/apparmor/profiles/"
"snap.<name>.<command>` or `/var/lib/snapd/seccomp/bpf/snap.<name>."
"<command>.src` to your interface code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:222
msgid "snap-seccomp versions and paths"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:224
msgid ""
"Tools such as snap-confine, snap-seccomp and snap-exec are internal to snapd "
"and are initially installed with a distribution's snapd package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:226
msgid ""
"On certain distributions, these tools can become superseded by versions "
"embedded in subsequently installed core and snapd snaps. When developing a "
"seccomp profile, it is important that the correct snap-seccomp binary is "
"used. This can be determined by inspecting which binary is running as "
"_snapd_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:228
msgid ""
"With re-execution from the subsequently installed core and snapd snaps, "
"these tools get called using their full path from the same location as the "
"currently running binary. This is visible from `/proc`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:230
msgid ""
"# with reexecution\n"
"$ sudo ls -l /proc/$(pidof snapd)/exe\n"
"lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 Jun  5 10:10 /proc/1994/exe -> /snap/snapd/7777/usr/"
"lib/snapd/snapd\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:236
msgid ""
"Thus tools such as snap-seccomp will be called using its full path, `/snap/"
"snapd/7777/usr/lib/snapd/snap-seccomp`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:238
msgid ""
"Without re-execution, the snapd process is using a binary located in the "
"host filesystem:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:240
msgid ""
"# no reexecution\n"
"$ sudo ls -l /proc/$(pidof snapd)/exe\n"
"lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 06-05 12:49 /proc/808335/exe -> /usr/lib/snapd/"
"snapd\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:246
msgid ""
"Correspondingly, `snap-seccomp` will be called using its full path `/usr/lib/"
"snapd/snapd`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:248
msgid "File permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:250
msgid ""
"While tradition file permissions are respected and enforced, any violations "
"are not currently logged. Similarly, device cgroups may also block access "
"without logging denials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:252
msgid "To check whether device cgroups are affecting a snap's device access:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:254
msgid ""
"see if there are any snapd-generated udev rules in `/etc/udev/rules.d/70-"
"snap.$SNAPNAME.rules`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:255
msgid ""
"if rules are defined, use `udevadm info /dev/$DEVICE` to see if the snap "
"shows up in TAGS, or see if the `/run/udev/tags/snap_$SNAPNAME_$COMMAND` "
"directory exists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:256
msgid ""
"examine if the `/sys/fs/cgroup/snap.$SNAPNAME.$COMMAND` directory exists and "
"if the device is listed in `/sys/fs/cgroup/devices/snap.$SNAPNAME.$COMMAND/"
"devices.allow` (eg, `/dev/kmsg` would have '`c 1:11 rwm`' since `/dev/kmsg` "
"is a character device with MAJOR:MINOR as 1:11 (see `ls -l /dev/kmsg`))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:258
msgid ""
"For device cgroups, create or modify `/etc/udev/rules.d/70-snap."
"$SNAPNAME.rules` as necessary (eg, `KERNEL==\"kmsg\" "
"TAGS+=\"snap_$YOURSNAPNAME_$YOURCOMMAND\"` would tag `/dev/kmsg` for your "
"snap), then run `sudo udevadm trigger --action=change`. To undo the access, "
"remove the file and run the `udevadm` command again. When done, update the "
"interfaces code based on your changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:260
msgid ""
"If you believe there is a bug in the security policy or want to request and/"
"or contribute a new interface, please [file a bug](https://"
"bugs.launchpad.net/snappy/+filebug), adding the `snapd-interface` tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/debug-snaps.md:262
msgid ""
"<!--\n"
"### Interface development and security policy\n"
"\n"
"When participating in snappy development and implementing new interfaces for "
"others to use, you will almost always need to write security policy for both "
"the slots and the plugs side of the interface but keep in mind you are not "
"expected to write perfect security policy on the first try. The review "
"process for snapd includes a security review of the interface security "
"policy and it is expected that the security policy will be iterated on "
"during the review process (in other words, if you are stuck on writing "
"security policy but the interface otherwise works, feel free to submit the "
"interface and ask for help).\n"
"\n"
"In addition to the above, here are some other useful techniques when "
"debugging/developing policy:\n"
"\n"
" * temporarily specify `@unrestricted` in the seccomp policy and this will "
"allow all syscalls\n"
" * temporarily use a combination of bare AppArmor rules to focus on only the "
"parts you want. For example:\n"
"\n"
"    ```\n"
"    file,\n"
"    capability,\n"
"    network,\n"
"    mount,\n"
"    remount,\n"
"    pivot_root,\n"
"    umount,\n"
"    dbus,\n"
"    signal,\n"
"    ptrace,\n"
"    unix,\n"
"    ```\n"
" * look at existing policy in `interfaces/apparmor/template.go`, `interfaces/"
"seccomp/template.go` and `interfaces/builtin/*` for examples of the policy "
"language\n"
" * [stracing snaps](https://forum.snapcraft.io/t/stracing-snap-commands/"
"1433). In addition to simply stracing the app, it can also be helpful to "
"strace the app in both devmode and strict confinement and comparing the "
"results.\n"
" * when testing new versions of snappy-app-dev, if re-exec is enabled you "
"will need to copy the new version to the location udev expects it (eg, `/lib/"
"udev`) and then bind mount it over where the re-exec'd snap-confine expects "
"it (eg, `mount --bind /lib/udev/snappy-app-dev /snap/core/<version>/lib/udev/"
"snappy-app-dev`)\n"
"\n"
"The above command has changed to snap-device-helper\n"
"-->\n"
